{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Mechanical per-surface follow-up to inadequate-hydrated-starling (aa0b-92f6-c132-4c23, merged as 90967aa7ff3d), which added the MCP-required tracker-op umbrella rule to `AGENTS.md` (the \"## Drive the tracker through the MCP server (local-code ops are the carve-out)\" section) governing every guidance surface by reference. This ticket does the textual sweep of the non-doc surfaces that starling's consumer scan enumerated.\n\n## Context / Problem\nstarling reconciled the guidance semantically at the umbrella level, but the individual non-doc guidance surfaces still instruct tracker ops as bare local `rebar <verb>` invocations with no pointer to the umbrella. A reader landing on one of those surfaces does not learn that, when an MCP server is configured, tracker ops route through MCP (the local CLI being the carve-out/fallback). This is the enumerated mechanical follow-up starling deferred.\n\n## Approach\nFor each in-scope surface, add a single one-line pointer at its tracker-op instruction(s) of the form \"route tracker ops through the MCP server when one is configured; the local `rebar` CLI is the carve-out/fallback for local-code ops\", cross-referencing the `AGENTS.md` umbrella section — without restating the whole rule (AGENTS.md stays canonical/lean). This is additive-pointer-only: no semantics change, and local-code-op instructions (`review-code`, `verify-commit-ticket`, `explain`, tests, git, `review-plan --status`) are left as-is or noted as the carve-out. One well-placed pointer per surface (at its primary tracker-driving section) matches the umbrella's \"governs by reference\" model and keeps each surface lean. Each pointer matches its file's existing voice/format.\n\n`templates/rebar-usage.md` is the byte-identical canonical source for the generated region in `templates/AGENTS.md` (guarded by `tests/unit/test_client_template_sync.py`), so the template edit is made in `rebar-usage.md` and re-synced into the delimited `<!-- BEGIN rebar-usage -->`/`<!-- END rebar-usage -->` region of `templates/AGENTS.md`. That test also enforces `FORBIDDEN_INTERNAL = (\"Gerrit\", \"module-size\", \"Serena\")`, so the template pointer stays client-generic and names none of those, and references the rebar MCP capability generically rather than rebar-internal specifics.\n\n## Scope\n- `.agents/rules/rebar.md` — the \"Track work in rebar\" bullet.\n- `templates/rebar-usage.md` — the canonical rebar-usage source (the loop / \"Working over MCP\" section).\n- `templates/AGENTS.md` — re-sync the generated region byte-for-byte from `templates/rebar-usage.md`.\n- `src/rebar/_guides/writing-a-passing-plan.md` — the loop that runs `review-plan`/`claim`.\n- `src/rebar/_guides/passing-code-review.md` — the bug-fix `review-plan`/`sign-review` block (leaving the `review-code`/`--status`/`verify-commit-ticket` local-code ops as the carve-out).\n- `examples/agent-skills/rebar-implement/SKILL.md` — the \"Discover the project's rules first\" section.\n- `examples/agent-skills/rebar-brainstorm/SKILL.md` — the \"Before recording tickets\" section (`create`/`link`/`ready`/`review-plan`).\n- `examples/agent-skills/rebar-debug/SKILL.md` — the tracker-writeback carve-out section.\n\nNo behavioral code changes; these are Markdown guidance files.\n\n## Testing\n- Run `pytest tests/unit/test_client_template_sync.py` — the template drift gate must stay green (the region in `templates/AGENTS.md` byte-matches `templates/rebar-usage.md`, and no `FORBIDDEN_INTERNAL` string leaks into the client template).\n- Run `make lint && make typecheck && make test` — all green; the .md edits are no-ops for typecheck/test apart from the template-sync unit test.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] Each of the eight in-scope files gains an additive one-line pointer at its tracker-op instruction stating tracker ops route through MCP when an MCP server is configured with the local CLI as the carve-out/fallback; verified: `git diff --stat` on merged commit lists exactly those 8 files (44 insertions, 2 deletions) and each added line was read.\n- [x] Local-code-op instructions (`review-code`, `verify-commit-ticket`, `explain`, tests, git, `review-plan --status`) are unchanged or explicitly noted as the carve-out; verified: HEAD touches no `.py` files and each diff hunk adds only tracker-op routing pointers, naming those local-code ops as the carve-out.\n- [x] `templates/AGENTS.md`'s generated region is byte-identical to `templates/rebar-usage.md` and contains no `FORBIDDEN_INTERNAL` (\"Gerrit\", \"module-size\", \"Serena\") string; verified: `pytest tests/unit/test_client_template_sync.py` passed (13 passed) locally and in CI.\n- [x] The full gate passes on the authoritative isolated CI runners: Gerrit change 2217 earned `Verified +1` (all lint/typecheck/pytest shards green, run 32843691099 = success) and `LLM-Review +1`. Locally `make lint` and `make typecheck` exited 0 and the template-sync test passed; the local `make test` had 3 unrelated git-isolation/tracker-footprint flakes (test_rebar_repo_isolation, test_tracker_footprint_cli, test_overlap_retrieve) from ~20 concurrent git-heavy sessions on this shared overloaded machine (load avg ~50-85) — HEAD touches no `.py` files, and the same full suite passed green on clean CI runners."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787660525293491001,"uuid":"bcbcb957-f24f-47da-af5c-2198d6aea127"}